{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>But if any man hate...</b> This passage alludes to what they said in the Gemara, Makkot 12a: three errors the prince of Edom is destined to err in the future to come, and one of them is that the cities of refuge will receive the unintentional killer even though he is deliberate. And it is known that the prince of Edom is Samael — he is Satan (Bava Batra 16a), he is the evil inclination, and concerning him the verse testifies here. And it says <b>if any man hate</b> — for there is no hater of a person like his evil inclination, and it is the source of hatred. <b>His fellow</b> — for it shows fellowship to all, for it counsels them for their benefit, yet it hates him and seeks his harm by way of love and fellowship. <b>And lie in wait</b> — that it lies in wait for him to cause him to sin, for you must know that the first transgression a person commits he does only as an unwitting act, and thereby the adversary finds a gate of entry into the soul. It also gets ahead of the person, as it says (Genesis 4:7) ״לַפֶּתַח חַטָּאת רוֹבֵץ״ — it arrives before the adversary's coming upon the person. It also puts a favorable face on the evil deed and calls the evil good, and there is no greater ambusher than this. And through this it overpowers him, which is what the verse means by <b>and rise up against him</b> — unlike beforehand, when it went within the person like a hair's breadth (Sukkah 52b), and through sin it overpowers and rises up against him." "<b>And smite him mortally.</b> Here the verse makes clear that it is speaking of Samael, and therefore it says ״and smite him mortally״ — meaning that it wounds the soul, for through sin the soul dies. <b>And flee to one</b> — meaning, if he flees to one of these cities to fortify himself, which is among the cities of refuge — <b>and the elders of his city shall send</b> — these are the heavenly court, which is the city of the prince of Edom. <b>And take him</b> — and according to justice they shall deliver him into the hand of the blood-avenger, who is our Father in Heaven." "And the verse's saying <b>and he shall die</b> — here is an allusion to the sod which they stated, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 38a): He extends a finger among them and burns them. And this is what the verse means by <b>by the hand of the blood-avenger and he shall die</b> — meaning, through the touch of the hand of HaShem upon him, he dies of himself. And because they said, of blessed memory, that the slaying of Samael will also be at the hands of Israel — that their hand will be upon him to kill him together with HaShem blessed be He — therefore it says <b>do not spare him</b>. And even though the righteous benefited through his intercession, they must look to the innocent blood — how much blood he shed in his ambush — and then <b>and it shall be well with you</b>: the aspect of the good-for-you will be permanently established, for when the spirit of impurity is annulled, the aspect of the good will be perfected in its place — and they are the children of Israel."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:14.372644359Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}